    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Oligarchy, Greek for “rule by few”, named the many Greek constitutions that kept power with the propertied; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> called it a corrupt form of government, rule by the rich for the rich.</li><li>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, whose Great Council was closed to new families from 1297, was the longest-lived oligarchy, governed by a hereditary patriciate until its fall in 1797.</li><li>Today the word is used loosely for the political power of great wealth, and Robert Michels’s “iron law of oligarchy” of 1911 held that all large organisations, even democratic ones, fall to a few.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>From 1268 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> elected its doge in ten rounds that alternated drawing lots with voting, from thirty members of the Great Council drawn by lot to forty-one electors, to defeat bribery and family factions.</p></aside>
    </section>

