    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">683/682 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> begins its yearly list of archons, an office then reserved for the well-born families, the Eupatridae</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 430 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>’s Histories the Persian Megabyzus makes the first recorded case for rule by a few of the best men</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">411 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The oligarchic Four Hundred overthrow the Athenian democracy, which is restored within a year</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">404 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> installs the Thirty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>; in eight months they kill about 1,500 citizens before democracy returns</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 330 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>’s Politics defines oligarchy as government by the wealthy in their own interest</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1297</span><span class="rf-text">The Serrata begins to close <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>’s Great Council to new families, creating a hereditary ruling patriciate</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1355</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>’s Council of Ten has Doge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marino-faliero">Marino Faliero</a> beheaded for plotting to make himself lord of the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1797</span><span class="rf-text">Facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>’s ultimatum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>’s Great Council votes the Republic out of existence on 12 May</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="works">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Works</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristotle.jpg" alt="Aristotle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">After Lysippos, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Oligarchy is government by a few, and in the Greek political vocabulary that gave it its name the few were usually the rich. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> argued that what made a constitution oligarchic was not the small number of its rulers but the principle on which they held power: wherever the wealthy ruled because they were wealthy, and in their own interest, there was an oligarchy, even if they happened to be many. In practice oligarchs were usually the propertied and the well-born together. Their instruments were property qualifications for voting and office, councils whose members sat for long terms or for life, magistracies open only to the few, and the exclusion or weakening of popular assemblies. Many Greek oligarchies kept an assembly but limited it to men wealthy enough to arm themselves, as the short-lived regime of the Five Thousand at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> did in 411 BCE.</p><p>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> showed how an oligarchy could be organised to last. Political rights belonged to the adult male nobles inscribed in the Golden Book, the Libro d’Oro, who together formed the Great Council, more than 2,000 strong in the 16th century. The Great Council elected almost all magistrates, among them a Senate of about 300 that handled finance, trade and foreign affairs, the Council of Forty, the highest court, and the Council of Ten, a security tribunal with secret procedures. At the head stood the doge, elected for life but bound by an oath, the promissione ducale, that listed the limits on his power. Most offices lasted a year or less and were poorly paid, and members of the Ten could not be related to one another, so that no single family could capture the state.</p><p>Oligarchy differs from aristocracy in its principle, wealth rather than merit, though most real regimes rested on birth and money at once, and from plutocracy, rule by the very rich, mainly in emphasis. It differs from monarchy and tyranny because power is shared within a group. Modern political scientists such as Jeffrey Winters also speak of oligarchs who hold no office at all but use great fortunes to shape policy inside democracies, and Robert Michels’s “iron law of oligarchy” describes something else again: the tendency of leaders to take control of any large organisation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Oligarchy emerged with the Greek city-state itself. By about 700 BCE most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polis">poleis</a> were governed by narrow circles of landowning families who had displaced or absorbed their early kings. At Corinth the Bacchiad clan ruled through a magistrate chosen yearly from its own ranks until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cypselus">Cypselus</a> overthrew it around 657 BCE; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> called their regime an oligarchy and noted that they married only among themselves. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where the list of annual archons begins in 683/682 BCE, the office was reserved for the well-born Eupatridae, whose rule modern historians describe as an oligarchy; the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a> in 594 BCE loosened it by making wealth rather than birth the qualification for office. The conditions were similar across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>: land was the main form of wealth, war was fought by those who could afford horses and armour, and law and religion lay in the hands of the leading families.</p><p>The word oligarchia, “rule by few”, appears in the 5th century BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> began to compare constitutions. In the debate on government in Herodotus’s Histories the Persian noble Megabyzus argues for entrusting power to a group of the best men, against rivals who speak for democracy and for monarchy. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> (431–404 BCE) the contest between oligarchy and democracy became a struggle across the Greek world, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> backing oligarchs and Athens democrats, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> described the civil war at Corcyra in 427 BCE, where each faction slaughtered the other. At Athens oligarchs seized power twice: the Four Hundred in 411 BCE, and the Thirty, installed by Sparta after Athens surrendered in 404 BCE.</p><p>The philosophers then defined the form. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>’s Republic presented oligarchy as the third of five regimes in the decline of the ideal state, a government of property qualifications in which the city splits into two, one of the rich and one of the poor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>’s Politics, drawing on a collection of Greek constitutions assembled in his school, placed oligarchy among the corrupt forms, rule by the few for themselves rather than for all, and analysed its varieties, from moderate regimes with low property qualifications to closed hereditary cliques. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> favoured a moderate oligarchy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> made oligarchy the degenerate successor of aristocracy in his cycle of constitutions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="history">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Through History</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-manin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludovico-manin.jpg" alt="Ludovico Manin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-manin">Ludovico Manin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lodovico_Manin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernardino Castelli, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Oligarchy remained the commonest constitution of the Greek world, and similar regimes appeared elsewhere. In northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the 6th and 5th centuries BCE the gana-sanghas, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavis</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a> and the Shakyas, the clan into which the Buddha was born, were governed by assemblies of the heads of leading families. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> discussed the constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, where wealth as well as merit counted in elections to high office, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>, for all its popular assemblies, was led by a small nobility of senatorial families.</p><p>In medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> oligarchy revived in the self-governing cities. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> a mercantile patriciate gathered in the Great Council stripped the doge of real power from the 12th century, and the popular assembly lost its role. The Serrata of 1297 enlarged the Great Council while closing it to new families; after the failed Tiepolo conspiracy of 1310 the Council of Ten was created to guard the state, and in 1355 it had Doge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marino-faliero">Marino Faliero</a> beheaded for trying to make himself lord of the city. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, after the revolt of the Ciompi wool workers in 1378, an oligarchy of great families led by the Albizzi governed until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo de' Medici</a>’s return in 1434. In 1528 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-doria">Andrea Doria</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> a constitution that reserved government to its enrolled nobility. Further north, Hanseatic cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> were run by councils of merchants who chose their own successors, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> a closed circle of patrician families, the regents, controlled the town councils and through them the provincial and general states.</p><p>The age of revolutions swept these regimes away. French armies overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> regents in 1795, and in 1796 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>’s Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> marched into neutral Venetian territory. Facing his ultimatum, the Great Council voted the Republic of Venice out of existence on 12 May 1797, and Doge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-manin">Ludovico Manin</a> abdicated; Genoa’s patrician republic became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ligurian-republic">Ligurian Republic</a> the next month. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> in October 1797 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> divided Venice’s lands, and the city itself passed to Austria. Oligarchy as a formal constitution, government by a legally closed body of families, did not long outlive the old regime.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="strengths">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Strengths</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xenophon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xenophon.jpg" alt="Xenophon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Xenophon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ancient writers who distrusted democracy made the case for moderate rule by the propertied. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> favoured a moderate oligarchy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> judged the regime of the Five Thousand, which replaced the Four Hundred in 411 BCE, the best government <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> had known in his lifetime, a moderate blend of the few and the many. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> held that a constitution mixing oligarchic and democratic elements and resting on citizens of moderate property was the most stable that most cities could attain. Property, its defenders argued, gave men the leisure to govern and a stake in good order. The argument long outlived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>: in the Putney Debates of 1647 Henry Ireton insisted that only men with “a permanent fixed interest” in the kingdom should choose its rulers, and property qualifications for voting rested on the same reasoning.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> became the model of an oligarchy that worked. Gasparo Contarini’s On the Magistrates and Republic of the Venetians (1543) praised it as a perfectly balanced constitution in which laws, not men, ruled, and admirers across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> repeated the praise. Its safeguards were real: offices were short, from 1268 the doge was chosen in ten rounds of lot and voting designed to defeat bribery, and the Council of Ten could condemn even a doge. No revolution overthrew the patriciate in the five centuries between the Serrata and 1797. The reports its ambassadors read to the Senate, the relazioni, were famous for their detail, and in 1606–1607 the Republic, advised by Paolo Sarpi, upheld its laws against a papal interdict. The patriciate was not wholly closed either: families that financed the state in war, as in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-chioggia">War of Chioggia</a> and the Cretan War, were admitted to the Golden Book.</p><p>Later theorists defended oligarchy as unavoidable rather than ideal. Gaetano Mosca and Vilfredo Pareto argued that an organised minority rules in every society, so that the real choice is between better and worse ruling groups. A small council, its defenders added, can deliberate in secret and act quickly in diplomacy and war, as Venice’s Senate and Council of Ten did for centuries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="weaknesses">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Weaknesses</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici.jpg" alt="Cosimo de' Medici" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo de' Medici</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cosimo_di_Medici_(Bronzino).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Workshop of Bronzino, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The case against oligarchy was made by the same <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> who named it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> warned that an oligarchy splits the city into two hostile cities, one of the rich and one of the poor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> observed that oligarchies fall either because they oppress the many or because the few quarrel among themselves. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a>’s reforms poor Athenians who could not pay their debts were sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and the resulting crisis nearly ended in civil war.</p><p>Greek history bore out these warnings. The civil war at Corcyra showed how far rival factions of the rich and the poor would go. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> the Four Hundred lost the loyalty of the fleet at Samos and fell within months. The Thirty, led by Critias, executed opponents, seized the property of citizens and resident foreigners and had even their own colleague Theramenes put to death; Aristotle’s Constitution of the Athenians puts the dead at no fewer than 1,500. Democrats led by Thrasybulus fought their way back, and in 403 BCE Athens restored its democracy with an amnesty.</p><p>Even <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> showed the costs. The Council of Ten and the three State Inquisitors worked in secret, using spies and anonymous denunciations, and the great majority of the Republic’s people, from Venetian artisans to the subjects of its mainland and overseas territories, had no political voice. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were confined to the Ghetto from 1516. As the patriciate shrank, many nobles fell into poverty, and the poorest, the barnabotti, were notorious for selling their votes. Venice’s economy declined from the 16th century as trade moved to the Atlantic, its government grew more cautious, and in 1797 it surrendered without a fight. Elsewhere oligarchies bred feuds: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>’s rival clans fought for generations and repeatedly handed their city to foreign lords, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>’s oligarchs, who exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo de' Medici</a> in 1433, were overthrown the following year. Modern critics such as Jeffrey Winters argue that great wealth still buys political power, chiefly to defend itself against taxation and redistribution.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Impact &amp; Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau.jpg" alt="Jean-Jacques Rousseau" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maurice_Quentin_de_La_Tour_-_Portrait_of_Jean-Jacques_Rousseau_-_adjusted.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maurice Quentin de La Tour (Life time: 1788), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The word outlived the regimes as a term of reproach, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>’s insight that wealth, not number, defines oligarchy shaped later thought. The image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> as a well-ordered republic influenced political writers from James Harrington, who borrowed its ballot for The Commonwealth of Oceana (1656), to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a>, who served as secretary to the French ambassador in Venice in 1743–1744, and John Adams, who studied it in his defence of the American state constitutions in 1787. Property qualifications for voting persisted into the 19th century: under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>’s July Monarchy (1830–1848) fewer than a quarter of a million men could vote. The historian Ronald Syme generalised the point, writing that whatever the form of government “an oligarchy lurks behind the façade”.</p><p>In 1911 the German-born sociologist Robert Michels gave the word a new meaning in Political Parties. Studying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>’s Social Democratic Party, he argued that any large organisation needs leaders, staff and specialists who come to dominate the members: “Who says organization, says oligarchy.” Michels later became a supporter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>’s fascism. His “iron law” provoked a long debate. Seymour Martin Lipset and his colleagues found in Union Democracy (1956) a printers’ union that had stayed democratic, and Josiah Ober has argued that classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, a large participatory democracy, disproves the law.</p><p>Today no state calls itself an oligarchy, and the word is used to analyse or accuse. In the 1990s it was applied to the businessmen who acquired former state assets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> during privatisation. Political scientists such as Jeffrey Winters use it for the political power of extreme wealth within democracies, and a much-debated study by Martin Gilens and Benjamin Page in 2014 concluded that economic elites and organised business groups had far more influence on American policy than average citizens, a conclusion other scholars have contested.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Oligarchy, rule by a few, usually the rich, was the commonest constitution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> and reached its most enduring form in the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, governed by a closed patriciate from 1297 to 1797. Defended as rule by those with a stake in order, it was condemned for dividing rich from poor, and its name survives as a warning about wealth and power.</em></p>

